<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abraham Accords</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abraham Accords are agreements, brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, by which Israel established diplomatic relations with Arab states, beginning with the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, which signed at the White House on 15 September 2020. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> followed later in 2020. They were the first normalisation agreements between Israel and Arab countries since the peace treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1979 and Jordan in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-accords.jpg" alt="Abraham Accords" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The White House from Washington, DC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_and_The_First_Lady_Participate_in_an_Abraham_Accords_Signing_Ceremony_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
